      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the given sum into this identity, we get:\n\\[\n(x + y)(x - y) = 12\n\\]\nSince we know \\( x + y = 6 \\), we can substitute this into the equation:\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\nTo find \\( x - y \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 6:\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
